<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Compose paints everything; this exists so the window has something before it does.

      The background matters, and it was wrong: the parent was the platform's dark Material theme,
      whose window is a mid grey, and it was the theme in both modes. Every launch opened on that
      grey and then cut to the app - white or near-black - which reads as a flash of another app.

      Light and dark follow the system here rather than the app's own setting, because this is drawn
      before a line of the app has run and nothing has been read from disc yet. A viewer who forces
      the app dark against a light system still gets one white frame; a fraction of the wrongness of
      grey for everybody.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Vayou" parent="android:Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/window_background</item>
    </style>
</resources>
